thermal-lab-cautions = In a real experiment use only a safe low voltage: a battery or a lab power supply. Never put mains (230 V) wires into water — it can kill.
thermal-lab-crystal-note-short = All the crystals have dissolved!
thermal-lab-crystal-note-long = The { $what } crystals that lay on the bottom ({ $c0 }) have all dissolved: at { $T1 } 100 g of water dissolves ≈ { $sol } g.
thermal-lab-crystal-note-short-2 = Part of the crystals has dissolved.
thermal-lab-crystal-note-long-2 = Part of the { $what } crystals dissolved: there were { $c0 } on the bottom, { $c1 } remain.
thermal-lab-crystal-note-short-3 = Crystals have come out!
thermal-lab-crystal-note-long-3 = there were { $c0 } on the bottom, now
thermal-lab-crystal-note-long-4 = now there are
thermal-lab-crystal-note-long-5 = The solution became saturated, and { $what } began to come out as crystals: { $c0 } { $c1 }. At { $T1 } 100 g of water holds only ≈ { $sol } g.
thermal-lab-crystal-note-short-4 = No crystals came out — the solution is supersaturated!
thermal-lab-crystal-note-long-6 = The solution cooled to { $T1 }, yet no crystals came out, though at this temperature the water holds only ≈ { $limit } of { $what }, and it has { $mass } — { $mass2 } times as much. The solution is supersaturated: the crystals have nothing to start growing on.
thermal-lab-step-outcome-word = in
thermal-lab-headline-energy-line = The heater released { $produced }; { $pct } % went into the contents of the vessel.
thermal-lab-headline-lines = highest
thermal-lab-headline-lines-2 = lowest
thermal-lab-headline-lines-3 = { $subj } will not { $notFuture }: the { $value } temperature it can reach is about { $limit }.
thermal-lab-headline-lines-4 = {" "}— and a red-hot one also radiates it away
thermal-lab-headline-lines-5 = Why: the hotter the body, the more heat it loses to the air{ $value }. At this temperature the losses equal the heat from the heater — and the heating stops.
thermal-lab-headline-lines-6 = Why: a body cannot get colder than its surroundings.
thermal-lab-headline-first = { $subj } will not { $notFuture }.
thermal-lab-headline-lines-7 = { $subj } does not heat up. { $reason }
thermal-lab-headline-lines-8 = The heater is off (now { $temperature }).
thermal-lab-headline-lines-9 = Why: the furnace heats the vessel from outside, so its walls get hot first — and this vessel is not made for such heat. Only a porcelain crucible goes into a furnace.
thermal-lab-headline-lines-10 = Why: there is no water to take the heat from the walls, and { $name } conducts it poorly — so the vessel itself gets hottest. For strong heating, use a porcelain crucible over a burner, or a muffle furnace.
## $gender — the Ukrainian name's gender (f, m, n).
thermal-lab-frozen = Frozen { $name }
thermal-lab-caution-flame = In real life { $lower } is never heated over an open flame: it catches fire easily and its vapour can explode. Fine in the virtual lab — not in a real one.
thermal-lab-gave-heat = To do so it gave { $heat } of heat to its surroundings{ $note }.
thermal-lab-stopped = The experiment was stopped after { $after }: the temperature changed from { $from } to { $to }.
thermal-lab-would-reach = If continued, it would { $future } in about { $after } more.
thermal-lab-cut-out = The { $lower } switches off as soon as the pan is hotter than ≈ { $cut }: its overheat protection, for a pan heated nearly empty.
thermal-lab-smokes = { $subj } did not { $future } but reached { $smoke } in { $at }.
thermal-lab-smokes-why = At this temperature it does not boil but starts to smoke and break down — heating further is dangerous.
thermal-lab-vessel-will-fail = The vessel ({ $vessel }) will not survive: in about { $at } its walls reach { $limit }, and then { $what }. By then it will not { $notFuture }.
thermal-lab-vessel-failed = The vessel ({ $vessel }) did not survive: in { $at } its walls reached { $limit }, and then { $what }. Meanwhile it only got to { $reached }.
